"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[7491],{7491:function(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"testId":"pharmacology-test-1","slug":"pharmacology","subject":"Pharmacology","testNo":1,"durationMin":40,"questions":[{"n":1,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"A patient presents to the emergency department with diarrhea, sweating, excessive lacrimation, and salivation. His pulse rate is 55/min and his plasma cholinesterase level was 50U/mL. Which of the following can be used to treat this patient?","options":["Neostigmine","Atropine","Epinephrine","Benztropine"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Atropine\\n\\nThe clinical features are suggestive of organophosphate poisoning and IV atropine is the treatment of choice.\\n\\nThe organophosphates commonly used as pesticides are anticholinesterases.\xa0Toxicity occurs by excessive inhalation of vapors, ingestion, or percutaneous absorption. It inhibits the cholinesterase activity which results in an increase of acetylcholine, leading to cholinergic symptoms.\\n\\nThe predominant initial signs are of muscarinic excess which includes:\xa0(DUMBBELSS)\\n\\n• Diarrhea\\n• Urination\\n• Miosis\\n• Bronchospasm\\n• Bradycardia\\n• Emesis\\n• Lacrimation\\n• Salivation\\n• Sweating\\n\\nThis may be followed by cognitive disturbances, convulsions, and neuromuscular blockade.\\n\\nTreatment:\\n\\n• Maintenance of vital signs particularly respiration\\n• Removal of clothing and washing of the skin\\n• IV atropine 2- 5 mg. If\xa0IV administration is\xa0not possible, then 2 mg IM\xa0every 5-10 minutes (till muscarinic symptoms disappear)\\n• Pralidoxime, a cholinesterase reactivator can also be used for treatment","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":2,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"A 60-year-old female patient presented with the following finding. Her medical history includes hypertension for which she has been taking regular medications for the past 3 months. Which of the following medication is most likely to be associated with this finding?","options":["Enalapril","Telmisartan","Amlodipine","Hydrochlorthiazide"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Amlodipine\\n\\nThe image shows pitting pedal edema in a hypertensive patient. Calcium channel blockers (CCBs) like amlodipine are most likely to be associated with peripheral edema.\\n\\nThe frequency of CCB- induced peripheral edema is both drug-specific (i.e more potent drugs like amlodipine will be associated with higher rates of developing edema) and dose-dependent (i.e incidence of edema increases with an increase in drug dosage and chronicity of its usage). It is more common among females.\\n\\nCCB-induced peripheral edema occurs due to a disproportionate decrease in the\xa0arteriolar resistance than in the venous circulation, resulting in increased\xa0hydrostatic pressure in the precapillary circulation. This leads to a fluid shift\xa0into the interstitial compartment causing edema.\\n\\nTreatment:\\n\\n• Dose reduction.\\n• Interclass switching of CCB - switch to non-dihydropyridines like verapamil, and diltiazem.\\n• Addition of a venodilator - ACE inhibitor, ARBs, Nitrates.\\n• Others - compression stockings, avoiding prolonged upright position.\\n• Definitive treatment would be discontinuation of the CCB and switching to another class of antihypertensives.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Common side effects of ACE inhibitors like enalapril include hypotension, hyperkalemia, dry cough, rashes, urticaria, dysgeusia, gastrointestinal upset, headache, and dizziness.\\n\\nOption B:\xa0Telmisartan, an angiotensin receptor blocker (ARBs), causes adverse effects such as hypotension, hyperkalemia, headache, gastrointestinal upset, and dizziness.\\n\\nOption D: Adverse effects of thiazide diuretics like hydrochlorothiazide are hypokalemia, hyponatremia, hyperglycemia, hyperuricemia, hypercalcemia, rashes,\xa0headache, gastrointestinal upset, and dizziness.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/71006fb7671c69dc66fc.jpg"]},{"n":3,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"A patient scheduled for a CABG procedure receives preoperative heparin for anticoagulation. During the surgery, the patient starts bleeding massively and there is a drop in BP. Which of the following would you use to manage this patient?\\n\\nCABG: Coronary Artery Bypass Graft","options":["Prothrombin complex concentrate","Protamine sulfate","Aminocaproic acid","Tranexamic acid"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Protamine sulfate\\n\\nProtamine sulfate is the drug used to treat life-threatening, heparin-induced bleeding.\\n\\nIt is a mixture of basic polypeptides isolated from salmon sperm.\xa0It binds to acidic heparin with high affinity and neutralizes anticoagulant activity. The resultant protamine-heparin complexes are cleared. Protamine binds only to long heparin molecules (UFH). Therefore, protamine only partially reverses\xa0the activity of LMWH and has no effect on\xa0fondaparinux.\\n\\nIt is most commonly used to neutralize heparin-induced anticoagulation after separation from cardiopulmonary bypass. It is also utilized in the setting of dialysis, invasive vascular procedures, and acute ischemic strokes.\\n\\nNote: Protamine sulfate has anticoagulant activity at higher doses and hence, only small amounts of protamine (1mg/100 units of heparin) are used for heparin-induced bleeding.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":4,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"A pregnant woman at 33 weeks of gestation presents to your antenatal clinic with preterm labour. Which corticosteroid will you administer for stimulation of fetal lung maturation?","options":["Hydrocortisone","Betamethasone","Triamcinolone","Methylprednisolone"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Betamethasone\\n\\nBetamethasone is indicated for the stimulation of fetal lung maturation in preterm labor.\\n\\nAs compared to cortisol, betamethasone binds to maternal proteins less and has less placental metabolism. This allows increased transfer to the fetus. Additionally, infants exposed to betamethasone are less likely to require postnatal resuscitation.\xa02 doses of 12 mg each are administered 18-24 hours apart when the delivery is anticipated before 34 weeks of gestation.\\n\\nDexamethasone is\xa0another long-acting glucocorticoid that can be given. It has benefits such as less risk of intraventricular hemorrhage and a shorter length of stay in the NICU.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":5,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"A 35-year-old lady presents with pulmonary TB is started on anti-tubercular therapy. What is the rationale for administering multi-drug therapy to her?","options":["To broaden antimicrobial spectrum","To reduce toxicity","To delay development of resistance","To prevent toxin release from the organism"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. To delay development of resistance\\n\\nThe reason for administering multi-drug therapy in tuberculosis is to prevent\xa0the development of resistance.\\n\\nResistance to individual drugs in Mycobacterium tuberculosis occurs by spontaneous point mutations\xa0that occur at a low but predictable rate (10-7 to 10-10 ). Development of this drug resistance is almost invariably the result of monotherapy,\xa0i.e., the failure of the health care provider to prescribe at least 2 drugs to which the tubercle bacilli are susceptible. Hence, multi-drug therapy is recommended for the treatment of tuberculosis.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":6,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"A 62-year-old woman presents with complaints of fatigue. She is a known case of type 2 diabetes mellitus on treatment. On examination, there is pallor and knuckle pigmentation. Which of the following drugs could have caused her condition?","options":["Metformin","Acarbose","Rosiglitazone","Gliclazide"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Metformin\\n\\nMetformin can cause vitamin B12 deficiency which manifests as megaloblastic anemia\xa0(pallor, fatigue) with knuckle pigmentation.\\n\\nThe most common side effect of metformin is GI intolerance as nausea, bloating, abdominal cramps and diarrhoea.\\n\\nNote: The exact reason why metformin causes vitamin B12 deficiency is not known.\\n\\nThe proposed mechanisms include:\\n\\n• Alterations in small bowel motility, which stimulates bacterial overgrowth and consequential vitamin B12 deficiency\\n• Competitive inhibition or inactivation of vitamin B12 absorption\\n• Alterations in intrinsic factor (IF) levels and interaction with the cubilin endocytic receptor.\\n• Inhibition of the calcium-dependent absorption of the vitamin B12-IF complex at the terminal ileum.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":7,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"A patient was given 200 mg of a drug that follows first-order kinetics. If 75 mg is excreted in 90 minutes, how much drug will remain at the end of 6 hours?","options":["6.25 mg","50 mg","30.5 mg","12.5 mg"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 30.5 mg\\n\\n30.5 mg of the drug will remain after 6 hours.\\n\\nIn first order kinetics,\xa0a constant fraction of the drug is excreted in a given time.\\n\\n75 mg of 200 mg is excreted in 90 minutes\\n\\n75/200 = 37.5/100 =\xa00.375\xa0is eliminated\\n\\nAfter 90 minutes; 200 mg-75 mg = 125 mg remains\\n\\nThe next 90 minutes (after 180 minutes) = 125 x 0.375\xa0= 46.875 mg is eliminated, and 125-46.875 = 78.125 mg remains\\n\\nThe next 90 minutes (after 270 minutes) = 78.125 x 0.375\xa0= 29.297 mg is eliminated, and 78.125-29.297 = 48.828 remains\\n\\nThe next 90 minutes (after 360 minutes) = 48.828 x 0.375\xa0= 18.310 mg is eliminated, 48.828-18.310 =\xa030.518 mg remains after 6 hours.\\n\\nA simpler way to calculate\\n\\n3/8 fraction is excreted in 90 minutes\xa0→ 5/8 remains after 90 minutes.\\n\\n6 hours (360 min) is 4 times of 90 minutes.\\n\\nSo 200 x (5/8)(5/8)(5/8)(5/8) = 30.518 mg remains after 360 minutes","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":8,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"A 30-year-old woman, in the first trimester of pregnancy from a tribal area, presented with fever and chills for the last 3 days. After examination of the peripheral smear given below, chloroquine was started but there was no improvement in the patient\'s condition. Which of the following is preferred for treating this patient?","options":["Quinine plus clindamycin","Quinine plus doxycycline","Artemether plus lumefantrine","Artesunate plus pyrimethamine-sulfadoxine"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Quinine plus clindamycin\\n\\nQuinine plus clindamycin is preferred for the treatment of chloroquine-resistant falciparum malaria in the 1st trimester of pregnancy.\\n\\nThe given clinical vignette is suggestive of a chloroquine-resistant\xa0malarial infection. The image given shows ring forms/ early trophozoites in erythrocytes, which are binucleate (double chromatin) and resemble stereo headphones in appearance, characteristic of malaria caused by P. falciparum.\\n\\nWorldwide most infections with\xa0P. falciparum malaria are resistant to chloroquine. In\xa0the 1st trimester, pregnant women diagnosed with uncomplicated malaria caused by chloroquine-resistant\xa0P. falciparum\xa0infection, the drug of choice is a\xa0combination of quinine and clindamycin. Artemisinin-based combination therapy can be used as an alternative for patients that are unable to tolerate oral quinine.\\n\\nIn\xa0the second and third trimesters,\xa0they\xa0can be treated with artemisinin combination therapy\xa0such as:\\n\\n• Artemether-lumefantrine\\n• Artesunate-amodiaquine\\n• Artesunate-mefloquine\\n• Dihydroartemisinin-piperaquine\\n• Artesunate + clindamycin\\n\\nDuring pregnancy, P. vivax malaria is treated with chloroquine as it is safe in pregnancy and children.\xa0In chloroquine-resistant vivax cases, clindamycin, quinine, chloroquine, and proguanil\xa0can be used.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: Doxycycline is useful in the treatment of malaria but not used in pregnancy.\\n\\nOptions C: Artemether plus lumefantrine is not the drug of choice in the 1st trimester of pregnancy. ACT may be given in the first trimester only if oral quinine is not tolerated or not available.\\n\\nOption D: ACT with pyrimethamine-sulfadoxine\xa0is avoided in the first trimester.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/e04ad56c29851edac510.jpg"]},{"n":9,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"A patient on long-term anticoagulation therapy with warfarin, would need an increase in dosage when coadministered with which of the following drugs?","options":["Captopril","Erythromycin","Cimetidine","Rifampicin"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Rifampicin\\n\\nA patient on long-term anticoagulation therapy with warfarin would need an increased in dosage\xa0when coadministered with rifampicin.\xa0This is because rifampicin is a potent microsomal enzyme inducer\xa0and can decrease the effect of warfarin.\\n\\nWarfarin is cleared hepatically primarily via the CYP2C9 enzyme.\\n\\nRifampicin induces CYP3A and CYP2D6 enzymes.\\n\\nEnzyme inducers:\\n\\n• CYP3A\\n\\n• Carbamazepine\\n• Phenobarbitone (CYP2B1)\\n• Rifampicin (CYP2D6)\\n• Phenytoin\\n• Glucocorticoids\\n\\n• CYP2E1\\n\\n• Isoniazid\\n• Chronic alcoholism\\n\\nNote:\xa0Isoniazid is an inducer for CYP2E1 but an inhibitor for CYP3A4\\n\\nEnzyme inducers\\nEnzyme inhibitors\\n\\nMnemonic: CRAP GPS\\n\\n• Carbamezepine\\n• Rifampicin\\n• Alcohol (chronic)\\n• Phenytoin\\n• Griseofulvin\\n• Phenobarbitone\\n• Sulfonylureas\\n\\nMnemonic:\xa0RED VICK\\n\\n• Ritonavir\\n• Erythromycin\\n• Diltiazem\\n• Verapamil,\xa0Valproate\\n• Itraconazole\\n• Clarithromycin,\xa0Cimetidine\\n• Ketoconazole","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":10,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"You read a news alert that mentions a novel drug has received marketing approval for diabetes mellitus and that it has shown positive results in a pivotal trial. This corresponds to which phase of clinical trials?","options":["Phase IV","Phase II","Phase I","Phase III"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Phase III\\n\\nPhase III trials are usually considered\xa0pivotal trials and\xa0are necessary for the approval of the majority of drugs.\xa0They\xa0are\xa0also called multi-site trials and are done for 3–5 years.\\n\\nWhen the initial Phase III trials are complete, the sponsor (usually a pharmaceutical company) applies to the FDA for approval to market the drug. This application is called either a New Drug Application (NDA) or a Biologics License Application (BLA).\\n\\nIn some drugs related to rare diseases or malignancies, phase II studies are considered as pivotal, and the drug may be approved on the basis of phase II studies.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":11,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"Which of the following antineoplastic agents is extracted from the plant shown below?","options":["Epothilones","Camptothecin analogs","Vinca alkaloids","Taxanes"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Vinca alkaloids\\n\\nThe image shown is of the periwinkle plant\xa0(Catharanthus roseus). Vinca alkaloids like vinblastine and vincristine are extracted from this plant.\\n\\nSources\xa0of few other drugs are:\\n\\n• Epothilones - Myxobacterium Sorangium cellulosum\\n• Camptothecin analogs -\xa0Camptotheca acuminata\xa0tree\\n• Taxanes - bark of the western yew tree.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/ab243907cd452fa5cab4.jpg"]},{"n":12,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"An elderly patient with ventricular tachycardia has been started on amiodarone. Which of the following is the most important adverse event associated with this drug\'s use?","options":["Pulmonary fibrosis","Central nervous system toxicity","Renal dysfunction","Ototoxicity"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Pulmonary fibrosis\\n\\nThe most important adverse event associated with the use of amiodarone is pulmonary fibrosis.\\n\\nAmiodarone is a class three anti-arrhythmic that is primarily used in the treatment of supraventricular arrhythmias, ventricular tachycardia and ventricular fibrillation.\\n\\nPulmonary fibrosis is usually seen in patients who take prolonged courses of amiodarone and in high doses.\\n\\nPulmonary fibrosis can be potentially life-threatening and requires immediate discontinuation of the amiodarone and initiation of high-dose corticosteroids.\\n\\nOther adverse events associated with amiodarone use are nausea, photosensitivity, corneal microdeposits and thyroid dysfunction.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: Central nervous system toxicity.\xa0There are no significant central nervous system-related adverse events. In a minority of patients, peripheral neuropathy presenting as shoulder and pelvic muscle weakness is seen.\\n\\nOption C: Renal dysfunction\xa0is not seen with Amiodarone since it is hepatically cleared.\\n\\nOption D: Ototoxicity is associated with prolonged aminoglycoside use and is not a feature of amiodarone toxicity.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":13,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"A 57-year-old woman is diagnosed with HER 2/neu positive breast cancer. Which of the following monoclonal antibodies is indicated for the management of this patient?","options":["Panitumumab","Bevacizumab","Trastuzumab","Cetuximab"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Trastuzumab\\n\\nTrastuzumab is indicated for adjuvant therapy in early and metastatic HER2 positive breast cancer.\\n\\nTrastuzumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody that binds to the extracellular domain of HER2/Neu.\\n\\nHuman epidermal growth factor receptor 2 tyrosine kinase receptor (HER-2-TK) inhibitors are:\\n\\n• Lapatinib\\n• Neratinib\\n\\nAnti-Her-2 monoclonal antibodies are:\\n\\n• Trastuzumab\\n• Pertuzumab","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":14,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"A patient diagnosed with peptic ulcer disease is being treated with sucralfate. Which of the following adverse effects can be seen in the patient?","options":["Increased risk of bone fractures","Vitamin B12 deficiency","Hypergastrinemia","Gastric bezoars"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Gastric bezoars\\n\\nGastric bezoar can be seen in a patient taking sucralfate. Sucralfate produces a viscous gel that has a ‘sticky nature’. The sticky nature may be responsible for gastric bezoars, particularly in those with gastroparesis.\\n\\nSucralfate is composed of a sulfate of sucrose with Al(OH)3.\xa0It\xa0undergoes cross-linking to produce a viscous polymer that sticks to epithelial cells and ulcers in acidic environments. It inhibits hydrolysis of the mucosal proteins by pepsin.\\n\\nIt is used in the prophylaxis of stress ulcers, and in conditions where mucosal ulceration is not responding to acid suppression.\\n\\nAdverse effects of sucralfate include constipation and bezoar formation. It may inhibit absorption of other drugs like digoxin, cimetidine, ketoconazole, and fluoroquinolone antibiotics due to the viscous layer formed in the stomach. Hence it should be taken at least 2 hours after administration if these drugs.\\n\\nThe other options:\\n\\nVitamin B12 deficiency and increased risk of bone fractures are side effects of\xa0proton pump inhibitors (PPIs). However, their clinical significance has not been proven yet.\xa0Hypergastrinemia is more commonly seen with PPIs than H2 receptor antagonists.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":15,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"The antiviral agent that is active against all herpes viruses and HIV is:\\n\\nHIV - Human immunodeficiency virus","options":["Foscarnet","Trifluridine","Acyclovir","Famciclovir"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Foscarnet\\n\\nFoscarnet is the antiviral agent that is active against all herpes viruses and HIV.\\n\\nFoscarnet is an inorganic pyrophosphate analog. It does not require activation by viral thymidine kinase for antiviral activity. It\xa0inhibits viral nucleic acid synthesis\xa0by directly\xa0inhibiting HSV DNA polymerase and HIV reverse transcriptase.\\n\\nFoscarnet is used to treat acyclovir-resistant herpes simplex virus (genital herpes) and varicella-zoster virus infections and ganciclovir-resistant cytomegalovirus (CMV) infections. It is also used as an alternative agent in CMV retinitis in AIDS, and CMV viremia in bone marrow transplant recipients.\\n\\nCommon side effects of foscarnet include nephrotoxicity and hypocalcemia.\\n\\nFoscarnet is mainly excreted by the kidneys.\xa0Therefore, dose reduction is necessary for renal impairment to avoid the accumulation of the drug and associated toxicity.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: Trifluridine is a pyrimidine antimetabolite antiviral drug. It acts against herpes simplex virus -1, 2, and CMV. It is approved for use in herpes simplex keratitis.\\n\\nOption C: Acyclovir is active against herpes simplex virus -1, 2, varicella-zoster virus, Ebstein Barr virus, and partially against CMV. It is useful against a variety of herpes simplex infections like genital herpes, mucocutaneous herpes, herpes simplex encephalitis, keratitis, and chickenpox.\\n\\nOption D: Famcyclovir is active against herpes simplex and herpes zoster virus. It is used as a second-line agent for genital or orolabial herpes.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":16,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"Which of the following statements about oral contraceptive pills is true?","options":["Progesterone is resposible for thromboembolism","It is protective against ovarian cancer","OCP are used in treatment mastalgia","Rifamipicin decreases the metabolism of OCP"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. It is protective against ovarian cancer\\n\\nOral contraceptive pills (OCP) protect against ovarian cancer.\\n\\nThis benefit is thought to be due to the prolonged suppression of gonadotropin stimulation of the ovary on long-term use of OCPs.\\n\\nOther benefits of oral contraceptive pills:\\n\\n• Lesser incidence of endometrial carcinoma due to progestin component in OCP\\n• Regularisation of menstrual cycles results in relief of dysmenorrhea and menorrhagia\\n• Reduced incidence of fibrocystic breast disease and ovarian cysts\\n• Endometriosis and pelvic inflammatory disease are improved\\n\\nOption A: The estrogen component of combined oral contraceptive pills is mainly responsible for venous thromboembolism.\\n\\nOption C: OCP is not used in the treatment of mastalgia.\xa0Mastalgia, chloasma, and breakthrough bleeding are known side effects of oral contraceptive pills.\\n\\nOption D: OCPs are metabolized by CYP3A4 enzymes. Rifampicin is a strong inducer of CYP3A4. Hence, if administered together, rifampicin can cause\xa0enhanced metabolism of OCPs and may lead to contraceptive failure.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":17,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"Which of the following drugs come under Schedule H of the Drugs and Cosmetics Act, 1945?","options":["Biologicals and special products like insulin, toxin and antitoxins","Bacterial vaccines","Drugs purchased with the prescription of a qualified doctor","Drugs with a warning label “Caution: it is dangerous to take this preparation except under medical supervision”"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Drugs purchased with the prescription of a qualified doctor\\n\\nSchedule H includes drugs purchased over the counter with the prescription of a qualified doctor.\\n\\nThe Drugs and Cosmetics Rules, classify drugs under different schedules based on their potential for abuse, their therapeutic value, and other factors. The rules provide guidelines for the storage, sale, display, and prescription of each schedule.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A- Biological and special products like insulin, toxin, and antitoxins are included under\xa0Schedule C.\\n\\nOption B- Bacterial vaccines are included under\xa0Schedule F I.\\n\\nOption D- Drugs that should be labelled with the words “Caution: it is dangerous to take this preparation except under medical supervision” are included under\xa0Schedule G.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":18,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"A patient who was hospitalized and started on an intravenous antibiotic has developed diarrhea. The findings of the colonoscopy are given below. Which of the following antibiotic is most likely to be responsible?","options":["Daptomycin","Fidaxomicin","Clindamycin","Chloramphenicol"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Clindamycin\\n\\nThe given clinical vignette of a patient with hospital-acquired diarrhea (nosocomial) following antibiotic administration is suggestive of\xa0pseudomembranous colitis\xa0most likely due to\xa0clindamycin.\\n\\nPseudomembranous colitis is an advanced form of clostridium difficile infection (CDI) that is visualized at endoscopy. Pseudomembranes are seen as whitish yellow plaques on the mucosa. Clostridium difficile infection is the most commonly diagnosed\xa0nosocomial diarrhea. It is commonly seen in individuals who receive a prolonged antibiotic course, that causes a change in the composition of gastrointestinal microbial flora. This leads to the colonization of C. difficile.\\n\\nAntibiotics that may cause CDI are:\\n\\n• Clindamycin\\n• Ampicillin\\n• Cephalosporins- cefotaxime, ceftriaxone, cefuroxime, and ceftazidime\\n• Fluroquinolones-\xa0ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin, and moxifloxacin\\n\\nClinical features include diarrhea, lower abdominal pain, fever, and leukocytosis.\xa0The diagnosis is based on clinical criteria:\\n\\n• Diarrhea without other recognized causes, and\\n• Detection of toxin A or B in the stool, toxin-producing C. difficile in the stool\\n\\nDiscontinuation of the offending antimicrobial, if possible, is the first step in management. Oral vancomycin\xa0and\xa0fidaxomicin\xa0(Option B) are used for the treatment of\xa0pseudomembranous colitis,\xa0depending on its severity.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Daptomycin\xa0may cause\xa0allergic pneumonitis\xa0in patients receiving prolonged therapy for more than 2 weeks.\\n\\nOption D:\xa0Chloramphenicol\xa0is mainly associated with adverse effects such as bone marrow suppression, and hypersensitivity reactions. When given to infants, it may cause\xa0grey baby syndrome.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/3e59c6a21389e1f2e142.jpg"]},{"n":19,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"A patient with recent-onset primary generalized epilepsy develops a skin reaction due to phenytoin sodium. What is the most appropriate course of action?","options":["Stop and restart phenytoin sodium after two weeks","Switch to ethosuximide","Switch to clonazepam","Switch to sodium valproate"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Switch to sodium valproate\\n\\nHypersensitivity reaction to phenytoin\xa0warrants discontinuation of the drug.\xa0Switching to sodium valproate is the most appropriate course of action for this patient.\\n\\nSodium valproate is one of the first-line agents indicated for GTCS. Clonazepam and ethosuximide are primarily used in absence seizures and are not indicated for GTCS.\\n\\nClonazepam is indicated as adjunctive therapy in the absence seizures when first-line agents (ethosuximide, sodium valproate, lamotrigine) fail to control the seizures adequately.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":20,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"A 54-year-old woman presented to the clinic with difficulty in vision. She is a known case of rheumatoid arthritis for which she has been on medication for many years along with drugs for the management of her diabetes and hypertension. Fundus examination of the patient shows the following finding. Which of the following drug could have caused this finding?","options":["Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs","Captopril","Sulfonylurea","Hydroxychloroquine"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Hydroxychloroquine\\n\\nThe given image\xa0depicts the bull’s eye maculopathy which can be caused by hydroxychloroquine.\xa0This patient\'s history of being treated for rheumatoid arthritis is the most probable reason for the lesion.\\n\\nBull\'s-eye maculopathy refers to the ophthalmoscopic appearance of a central area of retinal pigment epithelial depigmentation surrounded by relatively normal retinal pigment epithelium, giving a \\"bulls-eye\\" appearance to the macula.\\n\\nHydroxychloroquine, which is generally used for the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis, toxicity causes bull’s eye maculopathy. Chloroquine binds to melanin in the retinal pigment epithelium,\xa0causing\xa0cytotoxic effects.\\n\\nCauses of bull’s eye maculopathy include:\\n\\n• Chloroquine\\n• Hydroxychloroquine\\n• Congenital cone dystrophy\\n\\nOther adverse effects of chloroquine:\\n\\n• Nail bed and mucosal membrane discoloration\\n• Irreversible retinopathy\\n• Ototoxicity\\n• Toxic myopathy\\n• Cardiopathy\\n• Peripheral neuropathy","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/fa05f9ce9eb2d0ce54b6.png"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/5d98a2d06e0da1fe60a7.png"}]},{"n":21,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"A patient experienced two acute asthma exacerbations within one month despite ongoing therapy. Which of the following systemic steroids is suitable for her treatment?","options":["Beclomethasone","Budesonide","Prednisolone","Ciclesonide"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Prednisolone\\n\\nPrednisolone is a systemic steroid used for maintenance therapy after an acute exacerbation of asthma.\\n\\nIntravenous corticosteroids such as hydrocortisone are indicated if the lung function is predicted to be <30% or if there\'s poor response to nebulized β2\xa0agonists. After the exacerbation, oral predisolone can be substituted with maximal beneficial effect at 30-40mg daily.\\n\\nInhaled corticosteroids commonly used in asthma are:\\n\\n• Beclomethasone dipropionate\\n• Budesonide\\n• Ciclesonide\\n• Fluticasone\\n• Mometasone","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":22,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"A pregnant female at 36 weeks gestation, presents with palpitations, protrusion of the eyeball, and heat intolerance. On blood investigations, it was found that she had increased T3 and T4 levels and decreased TSH levels. Which of the following should be the next step in the management of this patient?","options":["Methimazole","Thyroidectomy","Propylthiouracil","Radioactive iodine"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Methimazole\\n\\nIn the given clinical scenario, the most likely diagnosis is thyrotoxicosis. As she is in her third trimester, the drug of choice for the treatment of thyrotoxicosis is methimazole.\\n\\nThyrotoxicosis during pregnancy is usually\xa0controlled by thionamide drugs. Propylthiouracil was\xa0preferred previously because it partially inhibits the conversion of T4 to T3 and crosses the placenta less readily than methimazole. However, since it has a higher risk of causing propylthiouracil-associated liver failure in pregnancy, methimazole is prescribed according to guidelines from the second trimester.\xa0Methimazole is usually avoided in the first trimester due to methimazole-associated embryopathy.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: A subtotal thyroidectomy is done once the hyperthyroidism is medically controlled. It is usually avoided during pregnancy, however, may be done in some cases where it can\'t be controlled via medication.\\n\\nOption D: Radioactive iodine is contraindicated in pregnant women as it may cause fetal thyroid gland destruction.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":23,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"A 60-year-old female patient was diagnosed with parkinsonism. She was commenced on combination therapy with co-careldopa. What is the rationale behind this combination therapy?","options":["To inhibit the metabolism of levodopa in brain","To decrease the plasma half life of levodopa","To promote the metabolism of levodopa in periphery","To reduce the systemic side effects of levodopa"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. To reduce the systemic side effects of levodopa\\n\\nThe rationale behind the combination therapy of levodopa with a peripheral DOPA decarboxylase inhibitor like carbidopa (co-careldopa) is that inhibition of the metabolism\xa0of levodopa (i.e. conversion of levodopa into dopamine) in the peripheral circulation decreases the systemic side effects of\xa0levodopa, like vomiting.\\n\\nPeripheral dopa decarboxylase inhibitors (carbidopa) do not cross the blood-brain barrier. Therefore, they only inhibit the conversion of levodopa into dopamine in the periphery and not in the brain. This leads to the increased plasma half-life of levodopa, and therefore, lower doses of levodopa can be prescribed.\\n\\nBenefits of combination therapy:\\n\\n• This therapy inhibits the metabolism of levodopa in the periphery, leading to increased plasma half-life of levodopa.\\n• Combination therapy reduces adverse effects like gastrointestinal side effects, orthostatic hypotension, and cardiac arrhythmias.\\n• This therapy allows for increased availability of levodopa in the brain.\\n• Fluctuation in motor control, where the patient is alternately well and disabled, occurs on prolonged therapy with levodopa. This type of \\"on-off\\" effect is reduced in combination therapy because of sustained dopamine levels in the brain.\\n• Pyridoxine enhances the peripheral metabolism of levodopa. Hence, combination therapy with carbidopa nullifies the interaction of pyridoxine with levodopa metabolism.\\n• \xa0A higher\xa0degree of improvement is seen\xa0in combination therapy than in monotherapy with levodopa.\\n\\nDemerits of combination therapy:\\n\\nThis therapy decreases the concentration of levodopa in the periphery, but increases the concentration of levodopa reaching the brain. Involuntary movements and behavioral adverse effects like confusion, hallucinations,\xa0nightmares, and delusions are not reduced after the addition of carbidopa. In fact, they are increased\xa0in patients who are taking levodopa with peripheral carboxylase inhibitors.\xa0This is because higher levels of dopamine are formed in CNS.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":24,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"A man presents to the casualty with fever and hematuria for 3 days. He also gives a history of recurrent left-sided loin pain radiating to the groin. X-ray KUB done is shown below. Which of the following is false regarding stones seen in his condition?","options":["Composed of calcium, ammonium, and magnesium phosphate","Rectangular or coffin lid appearance on microscopy","Increased risk with infections by urease producing organisms","It is formed in acidic urine"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. It is formed in acidic urine\\n\\nThe given scenario and X-Ray KUB showing staghorn appearance\xa0points to a diagnosis of struvite stones. Struvite stone is produced in alkaline urine.\\n\\nIt is commonly associated with\xa0infections with urea-splitting organisms like Proteus and Klebsiella species. Increased metabolism of urea results in alkalinity of the urine.\\n\\nStruvite is a triple-phosphate calculus (calcium,\xa0ammonium, and magnesium phosphate). The calculus may enlarge to fill most of the collecting system, forming a staghorn calculus.\\n\\nThese stones are dirty-white in color, and grossly smooth in texture. Rectangular or coffin lid\xa0appearance is seen on microscopic examination.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/4f1389f0cf4e321a3afc.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/628338a0c019ac0ef565.jpg"}]},{"n":25,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"The dose-response curve (DRC) is plotted for drug A both in the presence and absence of drug B. What type of effect does drug B have on drug A?","options":["Competitive antagonism","Noncompetitive antagonism","Agonism","Partial agonism"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Competitive antagonism\\n\\nDrug B has a competitive antagonistic effect on drug A.\\n\\nIn competitive antagonism, DRC shows a parallel rightward shift. Competitive\xa0antagonism occurs when the agonist and antagonist compete for the same binding site on the receptor. The response curve for the agonist shifts to the right in a concentration-related manner by the antagonist.\\n\\nOther Options:\\n\\nOption B: In non-competitive antagonism, there is flattening of the agonist DRC.\xa0Non-competitive antagonism\xa0effects occur when the agonist and antagonist bind to different sites on the receptor. ( allosteric site) and inhibits the response. The response curve\xa0progressively shifts in a\xa0right and downward direction until it reaches a limiting value when the allosteric site is fully occupied.\\n\\nOption C: In competitive agonism, the curve shifts to the left.\\n\\nOption D: In partial agonism, the\xa0partial agonist which has an affinity for the same receptor, also competes with and\xa0antagonizes a full agonist, while producing a submaximal response.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/0e1f8ae70ae3d49b280e.jpg"]},{"n":26,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"A 5-year-old child diagnosed with bronchial asthma requires bronchodilator therapy. Which of the following device will you prescribe for this child, to avoid oropharyngeal adverse effects?","options":["1","2","4","3"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 3\\n\\nThe device in image 3 is a\xa0spacer device that can be prescribed to\xa0decrease\xa0the incidence of propellant-mediated oropharyngeal adverse effects.\\n\\nSpacers reduce the velocity and size of particles entering the upper airways.\xa0This\xa0reduces the amount of drug depositing in the oropharynx and increases the proportion of drugs inhaled into the lower airways. Thus, a less amount of the drug gets swallowed and absorbed from the GI tract and limits local side effects.\\n\\nSpacer devices are useful in delivering inhaled drugs to children\xa0who are not able to use a pMDI alone but can also be used by adults for better drug delivery. The image below shows the use of a spacer device with a face mask which is preferred in children younger than 3 years of age.\\n\\nThe various devices used for the delivery of inhalational drugs are:\\n\\nPressurized metered-dose inhalers (pMDI) -\xa0Drugs are propelled from a canister in the pMDI with the aid of a propellant. These devices are convenient, portable, and can deliver 50–200 doses of a drug.\\n\\nImage 1 in the question stem shows a pressurized metered-dose inhaler.\\n\\nDry powder inhalers -\xa0these devices scatter the drug into a fine powder that is dispersed by air turbulence on inhalation. DPIs have been developed to deliver drugs such as insulin, but the dosing is inconsistent.\xa0Children below 7 years find it difficult to use a DPI.\\n\\nImage 2 in the question stem shows a\xa0dry powder inhaler.\\n\\nNebulizers:\xa0Two types of nebulizer are available -\\n\\n• Jet nebulizers\xa0that are driven by a stream of gas (air or oxygen)\\n• Ultrasonic nebulizers\xa0that use a rapidly vibrating piezoelectric crystal and thus do not require a source of compressed gas.\\n\\nThe nebulizers deliver much higher drug doses as compared pMDIs. These are useful in:\\n\\n• Treating acute exacerbations of asthma and COPD\\n• Extreme airway obstruction (severe COPD)\\n• Delivering inhaled drugs to infants and small children who cannot use the other inhalation devices\\n• Administering drugs such as antibiotics in relatively high doses\\n\\nImage 4 in the question stem shows a\xa0nebulizer.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/187efe4364e024fca27d.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/adaf45b90e89faafc251.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/23f37ee78b267f40dbae.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/15258f8f7acb1de0f048.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/a85b9dae452b326cafaa.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/9144290c25c1b313960c.jpg"}]},{"n":27,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"You are an intern and you see a 67-year-old male patient with a history of liver cirrhosis present to the emergency department with massive hematemesis. He is hemodynamically stable now after the first-line management. Which of the following agents are useful in the medical treatment of variceal bleeding?","options":["Pantoprazole","Octreotide","Somatotropin","Dexamethasone"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Octreotide\\n\\nOctreotide is a useful agent in treating variceal bleeding.\\n\\nOctreotide is a somatostatin analog and controls variceal bleeding by\\n\\n• Reducing hepatic and azygous blood flow\\n• Reducing hepatic venous wedge pressure\\n• Constricting splanchnic arterioles: direct action on vascular smooth muscle.\\n• Decreasing hyperdynamic circulatory syndrome of portal hypertension by inhibiting the release of contributing peptides.\\n\\nInitial management of variceal bleeding involves the following:\\n\\n• Admission to the intensive care unit\\n• Placement of\xa0two large-bore peripheral cannulae\\n• Blood transfusion\\n• Correction of coagulopathy\xa0with Vitamin K and tranexamic acid\\n• Octreotide, a somatostatin analog or\xa0terlipressin, a splanchnic vasoconstrictor\\n• Prophylactic antibiotic\\n• Endotracheal intubation for airway protection if bleeding is uncontrolled\\n• Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy\\n\\nUltimately, liver transplantation is the only way to definitively treat portal hypertension and variceal bleeding.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":28,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"A 65-year-old male patient complains of intermittent epigastric pain that is worse with food intake over the past 3 months. On further evaluation, he was found to have peptic ulcer disease caused by H. pylori. Which of the following drugs is not used in H. pylori eradication?","options":["Omeprazole","Mosapride","Amoxicillin","Metronidazole"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Mosapride\\n\\nMosapride is a gastrokinetic 5-HT4 agonist and is not indicated in H. pylori eradication.\\n\\nProton pump inhibitors like omeprazole or H2 receptor antagonists like cimetidine are usually present in anti-H. pylori regimens for reducing the pH of gastric secretions.\\n\\nAntimicrobials are used in the treatment of H. pylori infections as well. These include\\n\\n• Clarithromycin\\n• Metronidazole\\n• Tetracycline\\n• Amoxicillin\\n\\nH.pylori is a\xa0gram-negative\xa0motile rod with flagella. It has a unique feature of urease production, which\xa0is helpful in diagnosis. The\xa0sole source\xa0of H.pylori is\xa0human gastric mucus.\xa0In some patients, H.pylori causes\xa0mild acute gastritis\xa0lasting for about two weeks. In others, it can cause\xa0peptic ulcer disease, which may lead to chronic atrophic gastritis in later stages. This is a risk factor for gastric malignancies like\xa0adenocarcinoma\xa0and\xa0mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT) lymphomas.\\n\\nMany regimens generally indicated for eradication of H. pylori infections are given below.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":29,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"While conducting animal studies, a scientist is experimenting with a drug called vesamicol that causes skeletal muscle relaxation. What is the mechanism by which the drug causes muscle relaxation?","options":["Breakdown of acetylcholine after its release into the synaptic cleft","Blocks the transport of acetylcholine into its storage vesicle","Inhibits the uptake of choline into nerve terminals","Prevents the release of acetylcholine from nerve terminals."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Blocks the transport of acetylcholine into its storage vesicle\\n\\nVesamicol\xa0causes skeletal muscle relaxation by\xa0blocking the transport of acetylcholine into its storage vesicle.\\n\\nIt acts on the cholinergic nerve terminals and blocks the transport, thus preventing the repletion of acetylcholinesterase following transmitter release.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Acetylcholine esterase is involved in the breakdown of acetylcholine after it is released into the synaptic cleft to acetate and choline.\\n\\nOption C: Hemicholinum inhibits the uptake of choline into nerve terminals.\\n\\nOption D:\xa0Botulinum toxin inhibits the release of acetylcholine from nerve terminals.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/af82a98dd20386a9e9a0.jpg"}]},{"n":30,"subject":"Pharmacology","text":"A 28-year-old woman gave birth to a child with the features shown in the given image. On examination, the child had brittle bones, and x-ray scans revealed stippled epiphyses. Which of the following drugs is most likely to be implicated in causing this teratogenic condition?","options":["Lithium","Topiramate","Warfarin","Alcohol"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Warfarin\\n\\nThe given image shows a depressed nasal bridge (saddle nose). This image along with the stippling of bone epiphyses points to fetal warfarin syndrome or warfarin embryopathy.\\n\\nFeatures:\\n\\n• Repeated abortions\\n• CNS abnormalities\\n• Depressed nasal bridge/absent nasal bone/saddle nose at birth\\n• Bleeding diathesis at birth\\n• Growth retardation - hypoplasia of nose, eye sockets, and long bones with stippling of epiphyses\\n\\nAnticoagulation in pregnancy:\\n\\n• Warfarin: Although safe for the mother, it can lead to\xa0warfarin embryopathy, miscarriage, IUGR, and stillbirths.\\n• Heparin: It is safe for the fetus\xa0but is less effective than warfarin.\\n• LMWH: It is inadequate, and ACOG does not recommend its use in pregnant women with prosthetic heart valves.\\n\\nPeriod of gestation\\n\\nAnticoagulant\\n\\nTill 12 completed weeks\\n\\nLMWH or UFH\\n\\n13 to 36 weeks\\n\\nWarfarin\\n\\n36 weeks to 6 hours before delivery\\n\\nLMWH or UFH\\n\\n6 hours after vaginal and 24 hours after cesarian section\\n\\nLMWH or UFH (ACOG recommendation)\\n\\nIf delivery occurs while the anticoagulant is still effective, and excessive bleeding happens, then protamine sulfate is given intravenously.\\n\\nAnticoagulation of pregnant women:\\n\\n• The patient taking warfarin should discontinue it as soon as pregnancy is diagnosed and replace it with\xa0heparin 5,000 units twice daily subcutaneously up to the 12th week of gestation.\\n• Heparin is then replaced by\xa0a warfarin tablet of 3 mg daily\xa0to be taken at the same time each day and continued\xa0up to 36 weeks in patients with a high risk of thrombosis.\\n• Thereafter, warfarin is replaced by\xa0heparin, which is discontinued 6 hours\xa0just before delivery.\\n• Heparin (unfractionated or LMWH) is restarted 6 hours after vaginal delivery and 24 hours after the cesarean section.\\n• Unfractionated heparin (UFH), LMWH, and warfarin therapy are\xa0not contraindicated\xa0in breastfeeding.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/9a770121e0b74c9e9587.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/f8440fcd802785124089.jpg"}]}]}')}}]);